****************** G A M M A  P R O D U C T I O N ****************7231 1451   87
                                                                  7231 1451   88
Gamma production spectra in the MF6-MT102 files:                  7231 1451   89
                                                                  7231 1451   90
Below 1keV, the continuum part of the spectrum is calculated      7231 1451   91
by TALYS using the Composite Gilbert-Cameron model (CGCM)         7231 1451   92
as the level density model and the Enhenced Generalized           7231 1451   93
LOrentzian model (EGLO) as the strength function model as         7231 1451   94
advised by Kopecky-Uhl (Ref. 14). Discrete peaks are taken from   7231 1451   95
EGAF (Ref. 15).                                                   7231 1451   96
                                                                  7231 1451   97
Above 1 keV, both the discrete and the continuum part are         7231 1451   98
calculated with TALYS with the CGCM and the EGLO model            7231 1451   99
                                                                  7231 1451  100
************************* C O N T I N U U M **********************7231 1451  101
                                                                  7231 1451  102
This evaluated data file is based on a software system built      7231 1451  103
around the nuclear model code TALYS (Ref. 10), version 1.74. It is7231 1451  104
part of a large collection of isotopic evaluations, all created by7231 1451  105
running the system with either adjusted or default input          7231 1451  106
parameters. This means that the data in the entire collection have7231 1451  107
been tested in detail for many isotopes against individual        7231 1451  108
experimental data, while for other isotopes it is only as good as 7231 1451  109
the global quality of TALYS at the moment. The mutual quality of  7231 1451  110
all individual data files is however consistent: The same set of  7231 1451  111
nuclear models is used and, equally important, the same ENDF-6    7231 1451  112
formatting procedures for each isotope. The data file has been    7231 1451  113
created automatically using the ENDF-6 format generator TEFAL.    7231 1451  114
                                                                  7231 1451  115
The resulting data file provides a complete representation of     7231 1451  116
nuclear data needed for transport, activation, damage, heating,   7231 1451  117
radioactivity, and shielding applications over the incident       7231 1451  118
neutron energy range from 1.e-11 to 20. MeV.                      7231 1451  119
                                                                  7231 1451  120
All transport and activation data for neutrons, charged particles,7231 1451  121
photons and residual nuclides are filed. This includes cross      7231 1451  122
sections, angular distributions, double-differential spectra,     7231 1451  123
isomeric production, discrete and continuum photon production     7231 1451  124
cross sections, residual production cross sections and recoils.   7231 1451  125
This general purpose evaluation can thus be used as both          7231 1451  126
transport and activation library.                                 7231 1451  127
                                                                  7231 1451  128
A complete set of covariance data is included, which is stored    7231 1451  129
in MF31-40.                                                       7231 1451  130
                                                                  7231 1451  131
The OMP was adjusted based on parameters from G. Noguere to       7231 1451  132
obtain a good agreement with the (n,tot) data for natural Hf.     7231 1451  133
                                                                  7231 1451  134
***************************** T A L Y S **************************7231 1451  135
                                                                  7231 1451  136
TALYS is a computer code system for the prediction and analysis   7231 1451  137
of nuclear reactions. TALYS simulates reactions that involve      7231 1451  138
neutrons, gamma-rays, protons, deuterons, tritons, helions and    7231 1451  139
alpha-particles, in the 1 keV - 200 MeV energy range. This is     7231 1451  140
achieved by implementing a suite of nuclear reaction models into  7231 1451  141
a single code system. This nuclear data file is based on a        7231 1451  142
theoretical analysis that utilizes the optical model, compound    7231 1451  143
nucleus statistical theory, fission, direct reactions and         7231 1451  144
pre-equilibrium processes, in combination with databases and      7231 1451  145
models for nuclear structure. The following output of TALYS is    7231 1451  146
stored in this data file:                                         7231 1451  147
                                                                  7231 1451  148
- Total, elastic and non-elastic cross sections                   7231 1451  149
- Inelastic cross sections to discrete states                     7231 1451  150
- Exclusive channel cross sections, e.g. (n,g), (n,2n), (n,np),.. 7231 1451  151
- Inelastic scattering angular distributions to discrete states   7231 1451  152
- Exclusive channel energy spectra                                7231 1451  153
- Exclusive channel double-differential spectra                   7231 1451  154
- Exclusive gamma production for discrete states and continuum    7231 1451  155
- Elastic scattering angular distributions                        7231 1451  156
- Recoil data                                                     7231 1451  157
- Total particle cross sections, e.g. (n,xn), (n,xp),..           7231 1451  158
- Total particle energy spectra                                   7231 1451  159
- Total particle double-differential spectra                      7231 1451  160
- Isomeric and ground state cross sections                        7231 1451  161
- Residual production cross sections                              7231 1451  162
                                                                  7231 1451  163
For a description of the used nuclear models and their            7231 1451  164
parameterization we refer to (Ref. 11). If for this specific      7231 1451  165
nuclide we deviate from the global parameterization, it is made   7231 1451  166
explicit above under Nuclide Specific Information.                7231 1451  167
                                                                  7231 1451  168
